From db96c8efeb1dcb77b18402b0868b2c62ccc3b9df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Winter-Wijntjes <danielwinterw@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 18:11:39 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] skippy: batch activation exports by request offset, never by
 lane order

Batched exports are sliced by request offset. That is the microbatch row
order whenever llama decodes the batch with split_simple, which is what a
unified KV cache does. The old admission additionally demanded consecutive
lane ids in request order, a condition the scheduler's FIFO batches almost
never meet, so an exporting stage silently executed every lane as its own
graph.

Drop the ordering rule, and state the guarantee it stood in for: a batched
iteration that exports activations is one native microbatch.

- A per-stream cache splits by sequence, so an exporting stage with more than
  one lane needs a unified KV cache. Load rejects the combination once instead
  of failing every batched iteration, and the batched path still refuses it.
- Recurrent and hybrid memory splits an all-output batch by sequence
  (split_seq), where only the last microbatch's exports stay live. The runtime
  routes those stages one request at a time; the batched path rejects them.
- An exporting iteration that exceeds n_ubatch stays a scheduler bug.
- The export tensor's token axis must cover the whole iteration, so a stale or
  partial microbatch fails instead of slicing another request's rows.

Nothing in the batched path signals "unsupported" any more.
